.TH "CLOSEWATCHER.CLOSEWATCHER" "3JS" "March 2, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CloseWatcher.CloseWatcher \- CloseWatcher: CloseWatcher() constructor
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]CloseWatcher()\f[B]\f[R] constructor creates a new
\f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] object.
.PP
You can create \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] instances without user
activation, and this can be useful to implement cases like session
inactivity timeout dialogs.
However, if you create more than one \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] without
user activation, then the newly\-created one will be grouped together
with the last one, so a single close request will close them both.
This means that it is important to call \f[CR]destroy()\f[R],
\f[CR]close()\f[R], and \f[CR]requestClose()\f[R] properly.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
new CloseWatcher()
new CloseWatcher(options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An object that has the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]signal\f[R]
An \f[CR]AbortSignal\f[R].
If this is provided, then the watcher can be destroyed (as if by calling
\f[CR]CloseWatcher.destroy()\f[R]) by calling
\f[CR]AbortController.abort()\f[R] on the corresponding
\f[CR]AbortController\f[R].
.RE
.SS Return value
A new \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] object.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Creating new \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] instances
Create a new \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] watcher = \f[B]new\f[R] CloseWatcher();
.EE
.PP
Create a new \f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] with an \f[CR]AbortSignal\f[R] that
controls destroying the watcher.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] controller = \f[B]new\f[R] AbortController();
\f[B]const\f[R] signalWatcher = \f[B]new\f[R] CloseWatcher({ signal: controller.signal });
.EE
